v1.1: package LabWatch as an installable tool with a CLI and a VS Code extension

Core monitoring is unchanged. This release is about install, start, access and
daily use.

Packaging
- The backend becomes a real Python package: `labwatch` with the FastAPI app
  under `labwatch.server`, `labwatch/cli` for the command line, and the built
  dashboard bundled at `labwatch/ui` so an installed LabWatch serves the real UI
  with no npm, Node, or build step.
- pyproject.toml at the repository root (hatchling) declares the dependencies,
  the `labwatch` console script and the wheel contents. backend/pyproject.toml
  and the requirements files are gone; there is one source of truth.
- History now defaults to the platform data directory instead of a path inside
  the package, so upgrading LabWatch cannot delete a user's history.
- The frontend builds straight into `labwatch/ui`; CI fails if the committed
  bundle drifts from the sources it was built from.

CLI (standard library only, so --help and doctor survive a broken install)
- `labwatch` serves and opens the browser; `--demo`, `--port`, `--host`,
  `--data-dir`, `--no-browser`.
- `labwatch doctor` checks Python, dependencies, NVML, GPUs, the port, the data
  directory and the bundled dashboard, and names the fix for each failure.
- `labwatch start|stop|status|open|version`, with `--json` on status and doctor
  as the machine-readable interface the editor consumes.
- status is built for a one-line answer: "4 busy / 8 GPUs - 4 free", then one
  aligned row per GPU.
- Background start is portable (detached process, logged to the data dir); stop
  signals only the recorded PID after verifying it still looks like LabWatch, so
  a recycled PID is never killed.

VS Code extension (vscode-extension/)
- Status bar summary, a glance-only GPU sidebar, and Open Full Dashboard.
- It runs `labwatch status --json` rather than reimplementing the collector, so
  the editor and the dashboard can never disagree.
- Remote-SSH is the designed case: the extension host runs on the server, so it
  sees the server's GPUs, and `vscode.env.asExternalUri` asks VS Code to forward
  the dashboard port. No manual tunnel, nothing exposed on the server network.
- The formatting layer has no vscode imports and is unit tested under plain Node
  (13 tests), including malformed CLI payloads.

Fixes found while doing this
- The bundled dashboard made the SPA fallback answer unknown /api/* paths with
  HTTP 200 and a page of HTML, turning an API typo into a confusing success.
  Unknown API paths are now real 404s.
- The CLI printed a check mark to a GBK Windows console and crashed with
  UnicodeEncodeError on the happy path; it now detects what the stream can encode
  and falls back to ASCII.
- An f-string with reused quotes was a syntax error on Python 3.10, which
  pyproject claims to support. scripts/check-syntax-floor.py now guards the floor.
- scripts/check-imports.py caught 18 relative imports that were one level too
  deep after the package move; both scripts run in CI.

Tests: 179 backend (87% coverage, up from 122 at 59% before the CLI tests), plus
the extension's 13. New dev tooling: scripts/check-imports.py and
scripts/check-syntax-floor.py.
